1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
Same facts, two shapes. Turning three CSV tables into JSON for ticket 1001

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:10,100
The chapter's worked example: ticket 1001, users 101 and 201, events 5001 and 5002.

3
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:12,800
requester_id and assignee_id point at users by ID.

4
00:00:13,300 --> 00:00:15,300
Each event names its ticket with ticket_id. CSV text alone enforces none of this.

5
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:19,800
One JSON object holding three arrays: it mirrors the three CSV tables.

6
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:22,500
It is interchange text, not three MongoDB collections.

7
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:24,600
The ticket page starts at the ticket...

8
00:00:25,100 --> 00:00:26,900
...looks up users 101 and 201 to get their names...

9
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:29,000
...and finds every event whose ticket_id is 1001. Three places to gather.

10
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:34,100
Each user's name appears exactly once. A name change updates one record.

11
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:37,600
Now the ticket itself is the outer object.

12
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:41,900
Requester and assignee become nested objects, carrying copies of the names.

13
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:46,400
Events drop ticket_id: the containing ticket already says whose they are.

14
00:00:46,900 --> 00:00:49,100
Same ticket, same users, same two events and timestamps. One document read returns the page.

15
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:55,800
Access patterns from the chapter. Each shape makes different work direct.

16
00:00:56,300 --> 00:00:58,300
Embedding makes the page and one-ticket changes direct, single-document atomic.

17
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:01,200
Separate events support long history and cross-ticket reports, at the cost of a second query.

18
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:04,100
The question is not "which JSON is correct?" but "which reads and changes matter most?"

19
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:15,700
Remember: Keep the facts equal first, then compare the arrangement. Referenced: IDs carry relationships; each user's name appears once. Embedded: containment carries relationships; one read gets the page. Embedding copies shared facts and lets arrays grow. Ask which shape makes the important reads and changes direct.

